      "id": "hackernews-2026-05-17-mistral-s-ceo-europe-has-2-years-to-stop-becoming-america-s-",
      "title": "Mistral's CEO: Europe has 2 years to stop becoming America's AI 'vassal state'",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.businessinsider.com/mistral-ceo-warns-europe-2-years-avoid-us-ai-dependence-2026-5\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48170122\nPoints: 66\n# Comments: 71",
      "body": "Two years.That&apos;s the narrow window Europe has to build its own artificial intelligence infrastructure before becoming permanently dependent on American tech giants&comma; according to Arthur Mensch&comma; the CEO of French AI startup Mistral.&quot;It will be decided in the next two years&comma;&quot; Mensch said during a Tuesday hearing on digital sovereignty and AI at France&apos;s National Assembly&comma; translated by Business Insider.The 33-year-old cofounder of Mistral — one of Europe&apos;s best-funded AI startups and a challenger to OpenAI — said that the continent risks losing control over not just AI models&comma; but the energy and computing infrastructure powering them.&quot;Once supply is monopolized by American players&comma; suddenly we no longer have supply and we can no longer transform electrons into tokens&comma;&quot; Mensch said&comma; referring to the process of converting computing power into AI-generated output.He even said that Europe could eventually become &quot;a vassal state&quot; if it fails to develop its own AI industry and continues importing digital services from the US.Mensch has repeatedly touted sovereignty and Europe&apos;s independence from American AI companies as central to Mistral&apos;s open-source strategy&comma; saying recently that governments increasingly want AI systems they can control independently of US tech giants.The Paris-based startup has continued leaning into that message in recent announcements&comma; including a partnership with Groupe Caisse des Dépôts&comma; a state-backed French public investment institution&comma; focused on strengthening Europe&apos;s &quot;digital sovereignty&quot; through generative AI and GPU computing infrastructure.On Tuesday&comma; Mensch warned that the AI race is increasingly a battle over access to energy&comma; chips&comma; and data center capacity.US tech companies are already moving aggressively to secure those resources&comma; he said&comma; adding that Europe risks falling behind permanently if it moves too slowly.&quot;The Americans are deploying a trillion dollars next year&comma;&quot; Mensch said. &quot;The one who controls the chips&comma; who controls the electrons&comma; who has massive access to energy — that&apos;s the one who wins.&quot;Mistral&comma; which was founded in 2023 by former Meta and DeepMind researchers&comma; has emerged as one of Europe&apos;s flagship AI startups with a valuation of roughly $13.6 billion.Mensch said the company aims to build a gigawatt of AI computing capacity by 2029&comma; though he suggested Europe ultimately needs far more infrastructure investment.The executive also criticized Europe&apos;s fragmented regulations and capital markets&comma; saying they make it far harder for startups to scale than in the US.&quot;If we don&apos;t move fast enough&comma;&quot; Mensch said&comma; &quot;we&apos;ll end up in a situation where we have no choice left.&quot;&quot;In a world where you import all your digital services from the United States&comma; you have no leverage over the United States&comma;&quot; he added.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "Artificial Intelligence",
        "Europe"
      ],
